WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.266 --> 00:00:02.702 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
♪♪♪♪

00:00:11.945 --> 00:00:13.446 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
-[ Gasps ]

00:00:13.446 --> 00:00:16.783 line:92%
[ Train whistle blowing ]

00:00:16.783 --> 00:00:18.551 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
[ Heart beating ]

00:00:18.551 --> 00:00:21.087 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
[ Groaning ]

00:00:27.394 --> 00:00:29.929 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
[ Clock ticking ]

00:00:37.804 --> 00:00:38.471 size:88% position:44% line:92%
[ Click, ticking stops ]

00:00:38.471 --> 00:00:41.441
-Welcome back to "Late Night."
We're here with Joel Edgerton.

00:00:41.441 --> 00:00:43.076 align:start size:94% position:6%
Obviously,
this is a television show,

00:00:43.076 --> 00:00:45.678 align:start size:94% position:6%
but as the clip
would prove to anybody

00:00:45.678 --> 00:00:48.681 align:start size:94% position:6%
or anyone who's watched it
it's so beautifully cinematic.

00:00:48.681 --> 00:00:51.518
Did you even feel like
you were making a TV show

00:00:51.518 --> 00:00:54.287 align:start size:94% position:6%
or just feel like a
big, long movie?

00:00:54.287 --> 00:00:57.323 align:start size:94% position:6%
-It felt like, you know,
with Barry Jenkins at the helm

00:00:57.323 --> 00:00:59.426 line:92%
and James Laxton shooting it

00:00:59.426 --> 00:01:02.796
that it was like a 10-hour movie
that we were making.

00:01:02.796 --> 00:01:07.901
-Did you -- I mean, obviously,
you play a bad guy in this film,

00:01:07.901 --> 00:01:09.736 align:start size:94% position:6%
but, yet, I heard it was
a role that you

00:01:09.736 --> 00:01:11.771 align:start size:94% position:6%
sort of talked to
Barry about wanting.

00:01:11.771 --> 00:01:14.841 align:start size:94% position:6%
Do you like the challenge
of playing a villain?

00:01:14.841 --> 00:01:17.744 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Look, for years, I have bee
running around saying,

00:01:17.744 --> 00:01:19.279 align:start size:94% position:6%
"I really want to
play villains,"

00:01:19.279 --> 00:01:24.517 align:start size:94% position:6%
and I don't necessarily thin
I'm any good at it.

00:01:24.517 --> 00:01:26.186 align:start size:94% position:6%
You know,
the idea as an actor is that

00:01:26.186 --> 00:01:28.521
it's always going to be more fun
to play a villain.

00:01:28.521 --> 00:01:30.023 line:92%
And I heard about this project

00:01:30.023 --> 00:01:33.026 align:start size:94% position:6%
and basically stalked
Barry about it.

00:01:33.026 --> 00:01:37.163 align:start size:94% position:6%
And then I read the book,
and it's so --

00:01:37.163 --> 00:01:39.999 align:start size:94% position:6%
The character I play, anyway
not necessarily the show

00:01:39.999 --> 00:01:42.168 align:start size:94% position:6%
in its entirety,
but the character I play

00:01:42.168 --> 00:01:46.339 align:start size:94% position:6%
is so evil and dark
that I started to go,

00:01:46.339 --> 00:01:49.442
"Was this the right --
Should I have stalked him

00:01:49.442 --> 00:01:52.345 size:84% position:42%
before understanding
what the material was?"

00:01:52.345 --> 00:01:55.849 align:start size:94% position:6%
Because it's
a pretty heavy character.

00:01:55.849 --> 00:01:59.185
-Barry is a film director.
You're also a film director.

00:01:59.185 --> 00:02:02.655
Yet, you sort of went up
and introduced yourself to Barry

00:02:02.655 --> 00:02:03.857 line:92%
with the hopes of getting cast

00:02:03.857 --> 00:02:04.791 size:88% position:44% line:92%
Does that work with you?

00:02:04.791 --> 00:02:08.261 align:start size:94% position:6%
Do people ever, like, sidle up
to Joel Edgerton and say

00:02:08.261 --> 00:02:10.697 align:start size:94% position:6%
they want to do
your next project?

00:02:10.697 --> 00:02:14.200 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Look, I like ambitious people
You know what I mean?

00:02:14.200 --> 00:02:15.835 line:92%
Like, there's -- You know,

00:02:15.835 --> 00:02:17.270 align:start size:94% position:6%
there's a time
and a place for it.

00:02:17.270 --> 00:02:19.806 align:start size:94% position:6%
I don't know that I picked the
right time and place,

00:02:19.806 --> 00:02:23.543 align:start size:94% position:6%
like, an evening event
in Toronto,

00:02:23.543 --> 00:02:26.546 align:start size:94% position:6%
a couple of maybe
vodkas under my belt.

00:02:26.546 --> 00:02:28.715 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
But, you know,

00:02:28.715 --> 00:02:34.320 align:start size:94% position:6%
passion and ambition,
I think, are great traits.

00:02:34.320 --> 00:02:35.955 align:start size:94% position:6%
And, you know,
it's good to put yourself

00:02:35.955 --> 00:02:37.991 align:start size:94% position:6%
in a vulnerable position
and tell someone

00:02:37.991 --> 00:02:38.858 line:92%
that you really like their work.

00:02:38.858 --> 00:02:43.530 align:start size:94% position:6%
And, you know,
you've got to give it a crack.

00:02:43.530 --> 00:02:43.997 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
Crikey.

00:02:43.997 --> 00:02:47.367 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Do you feel like, you know,
as someone who does

00:02:47.367 --> 00:02:49.502 align:start size:94% position:6%
you know, both direct and act,
do you feel like

00:02:49.502 --> 00:02:51.571 align:start size:94% position:6%
when you work with different
directors you respect,

00:02:51.571 --> 00:02:54.207 align:start size:94% position:6%
you're always sort of taking
things with you

00:02:54.207 --> 00:02:58.878 align:start size:94% position:6%
for the next time
you go behind the camera?

00:02:58.878 --> 00:03:01.247 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Yeah. Look,
I think being on set

00:03:01.247 --> 00:03:05.018 align:start size:94% position:6%
is like a
place to learn from...

00:03:05.018 --> 00:03:06.719
As an actor, I learn from
directors I work with.

00:03:06.719 --> 00:03:08.988
As an actor, I learn from
other actors I work with.

00:03:08.988 --> 00:03:12.125 align:start size:94% position:6%
And, for example, in this show
I think it's also importan

00:03:12.125 --> 00:03:13.726 align:start size:94% position:6%
to understand that you can
learn from children.

00:03:13.726 --> 00:03:15.595 size:88% position:44%
I have this incredible
relationship in the show

00:03:15.595 --> 00:03:19.199 align:start size:94% position:6%
with a young actor called
Chase W. Dillon,

00:03:19.199 --> 00:03:21.868 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
who plays Homer,

00:03:21.868 --> 00:03:24.437 align:start size:94% position:6%
and you can
learn a lot from children.

00:03:24.437 --> 00:03:27.874 size:88% position:44%
I mean, if you keep your
eyes and your ears open,

00:03:27.874 --> 00:03:31.077 line:92%
you can steal from anybody

00:03:31.077 --> 00:03:33.246 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
[ Laughter ]

00:03:33.246 --> 00:03:36.516 size:81% position:41%
-Colson Whitehead --
-Looking at you, Seth.

00:03:36.516 --> 00:03:37.617 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
-[ Laughs ]

00:03:37.617 --> 00:03:40.053 align:start size:94% position:6%
Colson, who's been on our show
and, you know, obviously

00:03:40.053 --> 00:03:45.758
is brilliant, and I know,
from Barry, he visited set

00:03:45.758 --> 00:03:47.794 align:start size:94% position:6%
What's it like
when someone, you know,

00:03:47.794 --> 00:03:49.429 line:92%
who creates the source materia

00:03:49.429 --> 00:03:53.533 size:78% position:39%
comes on while you're
doing the adaptation?

00:03:53.533 --> 00:03:56.236 align:start size:94% position:6%
-When Colson came on set,
it was --

00:03:56.236 --> 00:04:01.608 align:start size:94% position:6%
For me, it felt like the C.E.O
had entered the building.

00:04:01.608 --> 00:04:05.011 align:start size:94% position:6%
There was an incredible amount
of respect, appreciation

00:04:05.011 --> 00:04:07.413 align:start size:94% position:6%
for what he'd created that
allowed us all,

00:04:07.413 --> 00:04:08.615 line:92%
hundreds of people, to be there,

00:04:08.615 --> 00:04:13.953 align:start size:94% position:6%
and a desire to want him
to feel good about everything,

00:04:13.953 --> 00:04:15.088 line:92%
like the sets, the costume

00:04:15.088 --> 00:04:15.989 align:start size:94% position:6%
And particularly for me,
I'm like,

00:04:15.989 --> 00:04:17.724 align:start size:94% position:6%
"Do I fill the shoes of
the character

00:04:17.724 --> 00:04:21.961 align:start size:94% position:6%
that you created in
your own private space?"

00:04:21.961 --> 00:04:23.296 line:92%
So it felt like, you know --

00:04:23.296 --> 00:04:27.700
It felt like a good, cool C.E.O.
had entered the building.

00:04:27.700 --> 00:04:29.602 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Alright, now,
I need to ask you this

00:04:29.602 --> 00:04:31.204 align:start size:94% position:6%
just because I know
you're back in Australia.

00:04:31.204 --> 00:04:36.276 align:start size:94% position:6%
How long has it been
since you were at the beach?

00:04:36.276 --> 00:04:37.910 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Two days.
-Okay.

00:04:37.910 --> 00:04:41.881
-Yeah, no, like, I go for
a swim or a surf most days

00:04:41.881 --> 00:04:44.817 align:start size:94% position:6%
but I have been
a little bit busy lately.

00:04:44.817 --> 00:04:46.886 line:92%
-Yeah. Fatherhood will do that

00:04:46.886 --> 00:04:50.023
It will take away -- It will cut
into your beach time.

00:04:50.023 --> 00:04:53.359 align:start size:94% position:6%
-It's about three minutes
that way on foot.

00:04:53.359 --> 00:04:56.029 align:start size:94% position:6%
And I have a garage
full of surfboards.

00:04:56.029 --> 00:04:59.465 align:start size:94% position:6%
Listen, man, we're very luck
here in Australia.

00:04:59.465 --> 00:05:01.301 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Yeah.
-We're able to move around

00:05:01.301 --> 00:05:03.469 align:start size:94% position:6%
The country's handled it
very well,

00:05:03.469 --> 00:05:04.170 line:92%
and we feel very blessed.

00:05:04.170 --> 00:05:07.106 align:start size:94% position:6%
And for that reason,
we're allowed to go and mingle

00:05:07.106 --> 00:05:09.375 align:start size:94% position:6%
at the beach and
go to restaurants and stuff.

00:05:09.375 --> 00:05:11.811 line:92%
So, you know, we're very lucky

00:05:11.811 --> 00:05:13.379 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Well, I'm very happy
to hear that.

00:05:13.379 --> 00:05:16.015 align:start size:94% position:6%
Please continue to
enjoy it all safely.

00:05:16.015 --> 00:05:17.150 line:92%
Congrats again on fatherhood

00:05:17.150 --> 00:05:18.818 align:start size:94% position:6%
and it's always
so nice to see you, buddy.

00:05:18.818 --> 00:05:23.156 align:start size:94% position:6%
-You too, mate.
It's always good to see you.

00:05:23.156 --> 00:05:25.258 size:78% position:39% line:92%
Thank you. Thank you.

00:05:25.258 --> 00:05:26.959 size:78% position:39% line:92%
-You're very welcome.

00:05:26.959 --> 00:05:28.394 line:92%
"The Underground Railroad"

00:05:28.394 --> 00:05:31.064 align:start size:94% position:6%
is now streaming on
Amazon Prime Video.

